WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.480
What do you think of, when I mention coffee&nbsp;
or the coffee house in a historical meaning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:10.400
You might think of the coffee houses in&nbsp;
the Arab muslim world with their initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.720
adoption of the beverage, or about the&nbsp;
first european coffee houses in Itally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.080
or about the Perisian coffee houses&nbsp;
during the age of enlightenment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.480
you might even think of the Loyds coffee house&nbsp;
in London which was the beginnings of one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.280
largest insurance and reinsurance companies in&nbsp;
the world. But, even though all of those coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.200
houses have interesting stories in their own&nbsp;
right, there is usually one aspect of coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.360
house history that is often overlooked and even&nbsp;
forgotten about by many people and that is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.600
historic Viennese coffee scene which arguably had&nbsp;
just as much influence on world’s history as did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.520
all the other coffee houses I have mentioned.
And it is due to this influence that the quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.200
“Viennese Coffee House Culture” is today part&nbsp;
of UNESCO as an “Intangible Cultural Heritage”.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.800
With all that said, this video is&nbsp;
sponsored by Blinkist which has some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.600
very interesting features but more on that later.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:05.360
So why did Vienna of all places end up with a&nbsp;
coffee scene so strong that it was later enshrined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.000
in UNESCO, after all it wasn’t the first city&nbsp;
in Europe to have a coffeehouse that title goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.320
to Venic, nor was it the second or third or forth,&nbsp;
Oxford, London, Hamburg, Paris, Marseilles all had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:23.840
coffee houses before the first one ever opened in&nbsp;
Vienna in 1683 well actually 1685, let me explain.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.560
The story goes that during the Ottoman retreat&nbsp;
after their second unsuccessful siege of Vienna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.640
in 1683 they left large amounts of coffee beans&nbsp;
behind. The Viennes were unfamiliar with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.080
new bean and didn’t know what to do with it.&nbsp;
In comes Kolschitzky an Austrian subject who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.840
had previously worked with the Ottomans&nbsp;
and therefore is aware of the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.400
these coffee beans could be used for making&nbsp;
coffee and so he takes those coffee beans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.400
sets up the first coffee shop in Vienna with&nbsp;
them and introduces the Viennese people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.680
the wonders of coffee making. At least that’s&nbsp;
how the myth goes. In reality the first coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.000
house in Vienna was opened in 1685 by two&nbsp;
entrepreneur Armenians Diodato and Luca.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.040
Nonetheless once coffee was introduced into the&nbsp;
city it started to slowly gain traction with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.320
local population who enthusiastically started&nbsp;
using the new coffee houses as a meeting place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.720
for simple discord or just a casual reading&nbsp;
space. This coffee house culture started to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.880
be more and more popular in the city when&nbsp;
in the 18th century it suddenly blew up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.080
A tourist visiting Vienna in the 18th century&nbsp;
wrote “The city of Vienna is filled with coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.520
houses where the novelists or those who busy&nbsp;
themselves with the newspapers delight to meet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.880
to read the gazettes and&nbsp;
to discuss their contents.”

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.440
These establishments, however, wouldn’t have&nbsp;
always looked the way you might be picturing them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.160
For one smoking was very common and some&nbsp;
cafes could have ended up looking very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.520
foggy although a fun anecdote is that it&nbsp;
was considered ungentlemanly to smoke in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.880
the presence of women. For example Princess&nbsp;
Pauline von Metternich an enthusiastic promoter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.000
of Richard Wagner and Betrich Smetana and a&nbsp;
regular of the Viennese coffee scene was onced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.040
asked by a man if him smoking would bother&nbsp;
her to which she replied “I don’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.920
nobody has ever dared smoke in my presence”.&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.120
But circling back, another maybe surprising aspect&nbsp;
of the early Viennese coffee house was billiard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.240
Billiard was in fact so popular in the Viennese&nbsp;
coffee house scene that some people just showed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.600
to the coffee house solely to see the best players&nbsp;
perform. In some instances these people showed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:31.520
at such high numbers that the floor collapsing&nbsp;
became an actual issue, after which in 1745 the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.040
Viennes police issued a prohibition on billiards&nbsp;
being set up anywhere else but the ground floor.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.400
With this boom of coffee house culture&nbsp;
in Vienna other kinds of coffee houses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.120
started to be established, ones that were a bit&nbsp;
more refined, ones that would have resembled a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.320
more quote “stereotypical” style cafe with&nbsp;
classical music playing in the background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.640
For example both Beethoven who was known to&nbsp;
compose in a coffee house and Mozart were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:00.000
known to perform in the Augarten Cafe, Strauss&nbsp;
sometimes performed with an entire orchestra in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.280
the Stadtpark Cafe and Schubert would often take&nbsp;
his friends to Cafe Bogner. With all that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.240
however, probably the most grandest of them&nbsp;
all was Cafe Schwender which started off as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.560
just a coffee house but later expanded&nbsp;
to a full size restaurant, ballroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.280
and even a music theater employing its own&nbsp;
entire orchestra to entertain the guests.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.000
There was also Cafe Kramer whose regulars&nbsp;
consisted of the University of Vienna professors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.120
and as such the cafe gained a reputation as&nbsp;
the quote “the scholarly coffee house”. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.480
was also the Museum Cafe where Franz Lehar and&nbsp;
Gustav Klimt used to hang out just to name a few.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.560
By the 19th century the coffee house became&nbsp;
the center point of the Viennes society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.440
and everyone from field marshals, writers,&nbsp;
journalists, aristocrats, philosophers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.760
painters and many more visited these&nbsp;
establishments on a daily basis.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.640
With so many high status people hanging out in&nbsp;
these cafes it meant that they also became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.600
center point of not just revolutionary movements&nbsp;
but also counter revolutionary movements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.880
Many Viennese cafes were involved in the&nbsp;
1848 uprising and after it many cafes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.640
filled with government spies listening to the&nbsp;
conversations being had. Scholarly Coffee House

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.560
With all that said, the Austrian coffee&nbsp;
scene continued on in its prominence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.720
probably the best quote to explain the&nbsp;
coffee house culture is by Stefan Zweig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.400
a well known Austrian novelist,&nbsp;
playwright, journalist and biographer.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:25.520
“Our finest place of education for everything new&nbsp;
was the coffee house. To understand this, one has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:31.360
to know that the Viennese Café is an institution&nbsp;
of a special kind which is not comparable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.880
any other in the world. It is actually a kind&nbsp;
of democratic club, accessible to anyone for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:42.160
inexpensive cup of coffee, where every guest&nbsp;
can sit for hours for this small obolus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.920
discuss, write, play cards, receive his mail&nbsp;
and above all consume an unlimited number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.800
newspapers and journals. In one of the better&nbsp;
Viennese coffee houses all Viennese papers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.160
available and not only the Viennese ones but those&nbsp;
of the entire German Empire as well as the French&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.800
and English and Italian and American ones, in&nbsp;
addition to all the important literature and art&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.160
magazines of the world, the “Mercure de France”&nbsp;
no less than the “Neue Rundschau,” the “Studio”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.360
and the “Burlington Magazine.” Thus we knew&nbsp;
everything that went on in the world first-hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.240
we knew of every book that appeared, of every&nbsp;
performance no matter where it took place and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:23.280
compared the critiques in all newspapers.&nbsp;
Nothing perhaps has contributed as much to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.240
the intellectual agility and international&nbsp;
orientation of the Austrian as the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:34.400
he could exhaustively find out in the coffee house&nbsp;
about all activities in the world and discuss them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:40.160
at the same time in the circle of his friends.&nbsp;
We sat there daily for hours, nothing escaped us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.760
For thanks to the collectiveness of our interests&nbsp;
we followed the orbis pictus of artistic events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:52.080
not with two but with twenty and forty eyes;&nbsp;
what one overlooked, the other noticed for him.”

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.400
This cultivation of thought in the Viennese&nbsp;
coffee house drastically affected Sigmund Freud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.200
the founder of psychoanalysis along with&nbsp;
many of his friends like Abraham Brill the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.880
first American psychoanalyst who all used&nbsp;
to frequent Cafe Herrenhof or Cafe Siller.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.520
On the other hand these same Cafes were&nbsp;
also frequented by Alfred Adler who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.400
instrumental in forming the psychotherapy school.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.440
By the turn of the 20th century Vienna had&nbsp;
around 600 to a thousand coffee houses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.400
it’s coffee house culture had spread all across&nbsp;
the Empire. Viennese style coffee-houses could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.320
be seen in Budapest, Zagreb, Prague, Krakow,&nbsp;
etc. and in one of these coffee houses in Krakow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.520
sat a man in exile, a man called Lenin. A man&nbsp;
who met another man to whom he referred to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:44.000
a "wonderful Georgian" a man known as Stalin.&nbsp;
Lenin sent Stalin to Vienna in 1913 to learn more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:49.920
about Marxism, and there Stalin met another man,&nbsp;
a man who frequented Cafe Centra, a man who liked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.040
playing chess in the Viennese coffee houses and a&nbsp;
man who later described meeting Stalin by saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:08:00.160
“He was short... thin... his greyish-brown&nbsp;
skin covered in pockmarks... I saw nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:05.200
in his eyes that resembled friendliness.”
This man was none other than Leon Trotsky.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:10.320
There’s a famous anekdote about Trotsky&nbsp;
where an official warned Count Berchtold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.760
foreign minister of Austria-Hungary,&nbsp;
saying that if a war was to start it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.240
might provoke a revolution in Russia or&nbsp;
even within the Habsburg monarchy itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:23.600
to which the foreign minister jokingly replied&nbsp;
"And who will lead this revolution? Perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.640
Mr. Trotsky (Bronstein) sitting&nbsp;
over there at the Cafe Central?"

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:30.960
It is almost certain that Trotsky took&nbsp;
Stalin to Cafe Central at some point but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.560
these two weren’t the only infamous&nbsp;
people visiting this establishment.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.920
An aspiring Austrian artist being rejected&nbsp;
from the Academy of Fine arts was also known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:44.800
to frequent many Viennese coffee houses&nbsp;
one of which was Cafe Central. Lastly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.560
it is often mentioned that Tito who&nbsp;
was also in Vienna in the early 20th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:52.320
century frequented Cafe Central but&nbsp;
I didn’t find any evidence for this.&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.040
No one knows if Trotsky, Stalin, Hitler and&nbsp;
even Tito happened to be in Cafe Central at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.080
the same time but it is certainly a possibility.
Karel Kramář and Masaryk the first Czechoslovak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.760
president were also known to frequent&nbsp;
Cafe Central when they were in Vienna.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.000
But Cafe Central didn’t only attract political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.280
figures it also attracted many writers, Cafe&nbsp;
Central along with Café Griensteidl was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:18.400
center of the “Young Vienna” movement that was&nbsp;
instrumental for many Austrian literary works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.960
Plus Franz Kafka was also known to frequent&nbsp;
cafe Central every time he was in Vienna.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.720
But with all this said you cannot mention the&nbsp;
Viennese coffee house especially in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.960
half of the 20th century without mentioning&nbsp;
the jewish aspect of the coffee house.&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.240
Vienna by the 20th century had&nbsp;
many aspiring jewish citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.280
who were instrumental in shaping&nbsp;
the Viennese coffee house culture.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:41.520
Hermann Bahr, Karl Kraus, Peter Altenberg,&nbsp;
Stefan Zweig, Felix Salten, Egon Friedell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.240
Alfred Polgar, Anton Kuh, Edmund Wengraf,&nbsp;
Sigmund Freud, and many many more were jews who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:49.120
actively participated in the&nbsp;
Viennese coffee house scene.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.480
Steven Beller in 2008 even gave a lecture on the&nbsp;
Viennese Coffee Scene titled “The [Viennes] Jew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:59.200
Belongs in the Coffeehouse” because that was&nbsp;
where in the first half of the 20th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:01.760
most of the Viennese jewsih history took place.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.400
All of this, however, is not to say that the&nbsp;
Viennese coffee house was just an upper class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:12.160
thing or only an intellectual thing, it permeated&nbsp;
all the social classes of the Viennes citizens.&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:19.040
For every Cafe Central there was a working class&nbsp;
cafe like Cafe Weghuber located in Viennas 7th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:24.160
district which at the time of the 19th century was&nbsp;
the center of the Viennes silk factory business.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:28.720
In this sense the Viennese coffee houses&nbsp;
weren’t just for gathering of intellectuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:33.600
they were places where young people used to gather&nbsp;
and flirt, where business meetings were conducted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.360
where illegal card games were played&nbsp;
in secret backrooms, where workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:42.240
decomposed after a hard day in the factory&nbsp;
and where political movements were organized.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.160
For example Cafe Eiles was the&nbsp;
center for the Austrian Nazi movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.920
and it was even the headquarters during the&nbsp;
unssescefule Austrian Nazi coup attempt in 1934.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.240
What makes the Viennese coffee houses&nbsp;
so crucial and so fascinating is that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.080
wasn’t just one social class or one&nbsp;
political movement that used them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.360
it was everyone, from the intellectuals to the&nbsp;
working class from the fascists to the socialist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.400
from the rich businessman to the pour factory&nbsp;
workers, all hung out in Viennese coffee houses.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.320
However, the Viennese coffee house&nbsp;
culture sufefred a very hard blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:18.240
after the second world war, not only&nbsp;
were many prominent members of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.880
social establishments killed during the&nbsp;
war or emigrated to the UK or the USA but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:27.040
many of the actual physical buildings where&nbsp;
destroyed during the bombings of the city.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.760
On top of that the post war politics didn’t at&nbsp;
first allow any large recovery for the Viennese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:36.800
coffee house scene as Austria found itself on&nbsp;
the borderlines between two major powers. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:41.120
political situation combined with the economics&nbsp;
of the coffee changing with the introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:46.880
of coffee chains, instant coffee and espresso&nbsp;
machines, exacerbated the worsening situation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:52.400
from the 1950s, the following decades became to be&nbsp;
known as the period of the "coffee house death".

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.240
With all that said, in recent decades&nbsp;
a renewed interest into the history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.520
of the Viennese Coffee house along&nbsp;
with rising tourism helped few of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:03.680
the old surviving coffee houses stay&nbsp;
afloat and start thriving once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:08.240
plus many new coffee houses got built with&nbsp;
the old Viennes coffee house culture in mind.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:12.320
Because after all the Viennese coffee&nbsp;
house isn’t just a simple coffee shop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:17.840
it is “a kind of democratic club, accessible&nbsp;
to anyone for an inexpensive cup of coffee”.

